-- The root cause behind the 2026-09-14 duplicate row, recorded as its own item
-- rather than folded into the pipeline item: the repair shipped, the design
-- flaw did not.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

insert into public.roadmap_items
  (phase, category, title, description, status, dev_percent_complete, is_public, display_order)
values
  (2, 'calculators', 'Battery scraper: key row identity on SKU, not source_url',
   'THE CIRCULARITY. upsertBatteries() in scripts/lib/scrape-common.ts matches an existing row on source_url, and source_url is ALSO a member of SCRAPED_FIELDS. So the gate can never propose a source_url change: a changed URL matches nothing, and "matches nothing" means INSERT. A vendor reorganising its URLs therefore forks a row every time, silently, and the field that would describe the move is the one field the review gate is structurally unable to raise. FOUND 2026-09-14 when EG4 moved the WallMount 280Ah All Weather page from /categories/batteries/eg4-wallmount-all-weather-battery/ to /categories/batteries/wpower-16-280-aw-powerpro-wallmount-all-weather/. The weekly run inserted id=34 as a "new" battery rather than proposing an update to published id=5, and both rows carried sku EG4LL48V100AODWMBV2. Repaired by migration 20260914000003; the mechanism that produced it is untouched. THE NAIVE FIX DOES NOT SURVIVE CONTACT WITH THE DATA. "Just key on sku" orphans 40% of the catalogue: of 25 rows, TEN HAVE NO SKU AT ALL and every one of them is Victron. victronenergy.com is one page for an entire product line with no per-model product URL, so each Victron row cites that model''s own datasheet PDF as source_url -- see the header of scripts/scrape-victron.ts. For those rows source_url is not merely the key, it is the ONLY identifier the source offers. sku is also nullable with no unique constraint, while source_url is not null unique, which is precisely why it became the key in the first place. THE FIX IS PER-RECORD, NOT GLOBAL: match on (brand, sku) when the scrape supplies a sku, fall back to source_url when it does not. brand is not null, and pairing it with sku stops a sku string colliding across vendors. THE REAL PRIZE IS NOT DEDUPLICATION. Once identity is sku-based, source_url becomes an ordinary proposable field and the circularity dissolves: a moved vendor page arrives in /admin/batteries as "source_url changed, apply?" -- one click -- instead of the hand-written data migration it required on 2026-09-14. That matters because actions.ts offers publish, unpublish, reject, apply and dismiss and NO FIELD EDITING, so today there is no in-app way to re-point a row at a URL that moved. SEVERITY IS LOWER THAN IT FIRST LOOKS, and the item should not be sold as a leak. findLikelyDuplicates() in src/lib/battery-review.ts groups by brand + voltage family + capacity rounded to 5Ah, and the admin query it feeds has NO is_published filter, so it runs across the whole catalogue: it would have flagged id=34 against live id=5 at review time. A duplicate battery does not reach a visitor unannounced. WHAT THE FORK ACTUALLY COSTS while it exists: updateScrapedFields() matches on sku, so the reseller scrapers write to BOTH rows -- price written in place on the unpublished twin, and a proposal raised against the live one -- plus queue churn and a human having to work out which row is real. THE WORK. (1) A partial unique index on (brand, sku) where sku is not null, enforcing what the matching code would then assume; safe to add today, the live board has ZERO duplicate skus since the 2026-09-14 repair. (2) source_url STAYS not null unique -- Victron depends on it. (3) upsertBatteries() chooses its match column per record. (4) The key-selection rule goes in src/lib so CI can test it with no service-role key, the same split that exists for scrape-health.ts and battery-revision.ts and for the same reason (rule 9). (5) Tests: sku-present matches, sku-absent falls back to source_url, and a moved URL on a published row produces a PROPOSAL rather than an insert. RESIDUAL RISK, accepted deliberately: Victron rows stay source_url-keyed, so a reorganisation of their PDF paths would fork all ten at once. That is loud rather than silent, which is the right failure mode for the case that cannot be keyed any other way. NOT URGENT. The review UI is a genuine safety net, so this buys back recurring manual work rather than closing a hole; the next vendor URL change is the natural trigger to pick it up.',
   'planned', 0, false, 77);
